ility that someone in n gets 10  tails  we'll now use this formula to solve both  parts  first we'll set n equal to 1024 which is  equal to 2 to the power of 10.  we substitute this in and we get the  following formula  we can actually estimate this without a  calculator  this term has the form 1 minus 1 over n  all raised to the power of n  if we take the limit as n goes to  infinity we get 1 over e which is  approximately 36.8 percent  we want 1 minus that so above will get  approximately 63.2 percent  it's nearly two out of three and that  ain't bad but it's not quite a  statistical certainty  so how large was nb to get to 99.9  percent  we'll set this equation equal to 99.9  percent  we'll then rearrange this equation and  simplify  we then take natural logs on both sides  and bring the exponent down  we then solve for n and then simplify to  get n is equal to 70 70 and some  fractional part  since n is a whole number we round up  we need at least  7071 people flipping coins to have a  99.9 chance that someone will get 10  tails in a row  i really enjoy problems like this and  i'm glad mark's tweet provided an  opportunity to cover this calculation  thanks for being the best subscribers on  youtube  thanks for watching and thanks for your  support  [Music]
 

